  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Widespread showers and a few thunderstorms through \nSaturday before drier and less humid airmass briefly settles over \nour region Sunday.\n\nMorning showers have greatly diminished the instability near Lake \nErie and far western NY today, with afternoon showers and isolated \nthunderstorms now blossoming across interior WNY and east of Lake \nOntario where limited instability is found. A weak wind flow aloft \nwill keep any thunderstorm that forms from becoming severe, but this \nflow may be just enough to maintain a forward propagation of storms \nto limit flooding potential through tonight. \n\nConvective activity will diminish this evening and early overnight \nwith waning instability. However a broad flow around a Bermuda \nsurface high will carry additional Atlantic and Gulf moisture from \nthe Southeast to the Lower Great Lakes by early Saturday morning, \nwith another round of showers reaching the western Southern Tier \njust before daybreak. PWats are not out of the norm for early \nAugust, with values around 1.5 inches, and this moisture combined \nwith MUCape values around 1K - 2K will result in a fair amount of \nshowers and thunderstorms for Saturday. There likely will be more \nthunderstorm activity tomorrow than today, owing to the deeper \namount of CAPE. \n\nA weak surface trough will pass across our region Saturday \nafternoon, that combined with an establishing lake breeze \ncirculation, will add additional lift for storms that are already \nblossoming in the peak heating of the afternoon. Severe threat is \nlow, with a deepening warm cloud depth to over 12K feet and 0-6 km \nbulk shear vectors generally in the 20 knot range. But rather the \nrisk for heavy rain and localized drenching downpours would be the \nbigger risk. \n\nStorms linger into the evening hours before a weak trough, and drier \nairmass follows later in the night. This drier airmass will bring \nless humidity Sunday to our region, though afternoon high \ntemperatures will remain similar.  \n\nKey Message 2... Unsettled weather remains for the Lower Great Lakes \nto start the new work week.\n\nA zonal flow early next week will begin to shift southward, with a \nmore noticeable dip late in the week as a deepening upper level low \ndrops southward from Canada. \n\nA moisture rich airmass will remain through the beginning of next \nweek, one that will support not only afternoon convection, but the \npossibility of overnight convection (Monday night into Tuesday), \nthough exact timing of subtle shortwave troughs is with still great \nuncertainty at this point in time. As the cooler and drier upper \nlevel low settles over the eastern Great Lakes region chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms will begin to diminish once the drier \nairmass becomes established.",
